-- title:
--   Cross-leg fibre description of the fine Čerednik–Drinfeld family
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $r$ and $\bar r$ are distinct primes, $N$ is a nonzero squarefree natural number divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$, and for $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ the integer $v(g) := \mathrm{toAdd}(\mathrm{vdet}\,g)$ is written for the value of the given monoid homomorphism `vdet`; by `hvdet`, $\mathrm{vdet}\,g = \mathrm{ofAdd}\,n$ holds exactly when $\det g = u\pi^n$ for some $u \in \mathcal{O}^\times$, so $v$ is the $\pi$-adic valuation of the determinant.
--
--   **Local data.** $\mathcal{O}$ is a characteristic-zero domain which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), $\pi$ an irreducible element, $\mathcal{O}$ is $(\pi)$-adically complete, $\mathcal{O}/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements and $(r) = (\pi)$; $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero fraction field of $\mathcal{O}$. $O^{nr}$ is a characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal{O}$-algebra with an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, complete for the ideal generated by $\pi$, this ideal being maximal (`hOnr_max`), with every element of $O^{nr}$ satisfying a monic $\mathcal{O}$-polynomial modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial of positive degree having a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^r \pmod{\pi}$ (`hFr`).
--
--   **Quaternion algebras and moduli.** $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ is indefinite and ramified exactly at the primes above $r$ and $\bar r$ (`hB`), $\Lambda$ is a maximal $\mathbb{Z}$-order in it, and all rational integers lie in $\Lambda$ (`hΛℤ`). The scheme $\mathcal{X}$ over $\mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal{O}$ with point maps `pt` is a coarse moduli space for fake elliptic curves with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ structure (`h𝒳`). The auxiliary level is $n \ge 3$, prime to $r$, to $\bar r$ and to $N$; $M \to \mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal{O}$ with `ptF` is a fine moduli space for such curves with full level-$n$ structure (`hM`), and $\rho : G \to \mathrm{Aut}\,M$, $\chi : G \to \Lambda$ constitute a level-twist action (`hρ`): $\rho$ preserves the base, twisting a full level structure by $\chi(g)$ is realised by $\rho(g)$, and $\chi$ is multiplicative, surjective and injective modulo $n$. The morphism $p : M \to \mathcal{X}$ is compatible with the structure maps (`hp`), invariant under $\rho$ (`hρp`), and forgets the level-$n$ structure on points (`hp_pt`).
--
--   **Hecke and Atkin–Lehner data.** For each prime $\ell \neq r, \bar r$, $\mathcal{Y}_\ell \to \mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal{O}$ with `ptT` is a coarse moduli space for curves with extra level $\ell$ (`h𝒴`), and $d_0, d_1 : \mathcal{Y}_\ell \to \mathcal{X}$ lie over $\mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal{O}$ (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`) and are given on points by forgetting the extra level (`hd₀`) and by passing to the quotient by a level-$\ell$ isogeny (`hd₁`). The endomorphisms $a_r, a_{\bar r}$ of $\mathcal{X}$ lie over the base (`harf`, `harbarf`) and are described on points by the Atkin–Lehner quotient relations at $r$ and at $\bar r$ (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   **The definite side and the group $\tilde\Gamma$.** $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a_1,b_1]$ is definite and ramified exactly at $\bar r$ (`hdef`), $\Lambda_1$ is maximal, $R_1 \le \Lambda_1$ is an Eichler order of level $N$, $n_1$ lies in the prime Hecke set of $R_1$ at $r$ (`hn₁`) and the intersected order $R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ is Eichler of level $Nr$ (`hS₁`); $\iota_0$ is an injective $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra embedding into $M_2(K_0)$, and $v$ is the prime of $\mathbb{Z}$ above $r$. The subgroup $\tilde\Gamma = \Gamma t$ consists exactly of the units integral away from $v$ in the sense of `awayUnits R₁ v` (`hΓt`). Elements $s_\ell$ and adelic elements $sf_\ell$ are given, subject to `hs` (local shape of $sf_\ell$ away from and at $r$, membership of $\ell \cdot sf_\ell^{-1}$ in the level Hecke set or prime Hecke set of the intersected order according to whether $\ell \mid N$, and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_\ell) = \ell$), with $\Gamma_\ell = \tilde\Gamma \cap s_\ell \tilde\Gamma s_\ell^{-1}$ (`hΓtℓ`); $\bar w$ is a unit of reduced norm $\bar r$ normalising $\tilde\Gamma$ (`hwbar`). The map `coord` is an order coordinatisation of $\Lambda$ in $\mathbb{Z}_r$-Witt coordinates (`hcoord`: additivity, the twisted multiplication rule, injectivity, density and the trace condition).
--
--   **Special-fibre uniformisation data.** Over $O^{nr}/(\pi)$ there are a fake elliptic curve $A_0$, a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $X_0$ of height $4$ (`hX₀`), formal coordinates $\theta_0$ of $A_0$ with $A_0$ a formal module via `coord`, $X_0$, $\theta_0$ (`hA₀`), and families $e, e' : \tilde\Gamma \to \mathrm{End}(A_0)$ over the base with degrees `deg`. The hypotheses `hE1`, `hE1mul`, `hE1sc`, `hE1'`, `hE2`, `hE3`, `hE4` (summarised here) state that each $e_\gamma$ is one half of an isogeny pair of degree $r^{\deg\gamma}$ preserving the level structure, that $e$ is multiplicative and sends rational scalars to scalars up to $r$-powers, that only scalars act by scalars, that $e$ is realised after any injective matrix realisation $E_0$ of the centraliser of the $X_0$-action by $r^{k_\gamma} g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$ for a single $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$, that $e$ acts on each full level-$n$ structure through labels in $\Lambda$ that are multiplicative modulo $n$, and that over any algebraically closed extension every $r$-power isogeny self-map of a pullback of $A_0$ preserving the level comes from some $e_\gamma$ up to $r$-powers. The hypotheses `hR₂`, `hR₂o`, `hR₂r`, `hê`, `hE5a`, `hE5b1`, `hE5b2`, `hE5b3`, `hE5c`, `hE5d`, `hE5e` (summarised here) extend this to an order $R_2 \le R_1$ with $r$-power denominators, acting on $A_0$ through $\hat e$ compatibly with the group law, the $\Lambda$-action and the level, multiplicatively, sending integers to scalars, sending $x^\ast x$ to multiplication by $\mathrm{nrd}(x)$, agreeing with $e$ on $r$-power multiples of elements of $\tilde\Gamma$, and realised simultaneously with $e$ by a single $g$ via $E_0$.
--
--   **The formal moduli package.** $\iota : \mathbb{Z}_{r,2} \to O^{nr}$ is a ring map, $\Phi$ is a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over $O^{nr}/(r)$ which is special for the structure map induced by $\iota$ (`hΦ`) and of height $4$ (`hΦ4`), $MD$ is a moduli package which is a Zariski sheaf (`hMD`), and $\eta$ assigns to rigidified objects points of $MD$, subject to `hη` (three clauses: on admissible objects $\eta$ separates exactly isomorphism classes; $\eta$ is natural in the base ring; and every point of $MD$ is locally in the image on a Zariski cover). $E_0$ is an injective ring map from the centraliser of the $\Phi$-action into $M_2(K_0)$ with bounded denominators in both directions (`hE₀`). The natural family $eD$, with `hnatD`, `hbijD`, `hfstD`, `hGLD`, `hPiD`, `hPiexD`, identifies the $G$-points of $MD$ over $\mathcal{O}$ bijectively and naturally with pairs consisting of an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $O^{nr} \to B$ (the leg) and a Deligne datum in $\Omega(K_0,\pi)(B)$, compatibly with legs, carrying the relation `ModuliPackage.G.IsActBy` into the twisted action `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct`, carrying $\pi$-translation into the Frobenius twist of the leg, and with $\pi$-translates always existing. Further, $\kappa$ with `hκ` compares the two residue rings and $\beta_0$ is an isogeny from $\Phi$ to $X_0 \otimes_\kappa$ of height $4n_0$ (`hβ₀`), and $P_0$ is a full level-$n$ structure on $A_0$.
--
--   **Atkin–Lehner data in the special fibre.** $A_{0w}$, $a_w$, $a_w'$, $b_w$, $b_w'$ with `hALw` (an Atkin–Lehner quotient at $\bar r$ via $a_w, a_w'$), `hBSw` (isogeny pair of degree $r^{k_w}$), `hBSwlev` (level preservation) and `hJOINTw` (a single $g$ realising simultaneously all $e_\gamma$ by $r^{k_\gamma} g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$ and the composite $a_w \circ b_w$ by $r^{k_w'} g\,\iota_0(\bar w)\,g^{-1}$).
--
--   **The comparison map $\Xi$ and the point-level dictionary.** $\Xi$ assigns to each rigidified curve over a Noetherian $\pi$-nilpotent $\mathcal{O}$-algebra a $G$-point of $MD$, subject to `hΞleg` (on connected base the leg of $\Xi$ is a Frobenius twist of $\psi$), `hΞnat` (naturality under pullback of curve and rigidification), `hΞiso` (invariance under isomorphisms of rigidified curves up to $r$-powers) and `hΞdef` (the formula for $\Xi$: it is $\eta$ applied to a rigidified formal module transported from $\theta_0$ through $\kappa, \beta_0$ with twist index $j \le 1$, on the Frobenius-twisted leg). With a fixed $g_0 \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$, `heqΓ` and `heqW` state that translating a rigidification by $e_\gamma$, resp. by $a_w \circ b_w$, moves $\Xi$ by the element $r^c\,(g_0\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g_0^{-1})$, resp. $r^c\,(g_0\,\iota_0(\bar w)\,g_0^{-1})$, in the sense of `ModuliPackage.G.IsActBy`. The hypotheses `hK1`–`hK4` give the point-level dictionary for the normalised leg $\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-v(g_0)}$: existence of a curve with rigidification matching any prescribed pair (leg, Deligne datum) under the $g_0^{-1}$-twisted action (`hK1`), uniqueness up to an isomorphism of rigidified curves (`hK2`), existence of pullbacks along $\mathcal{O}$-algebra maps (`hK3`), and a lifting statement along surjections whose kernel squares to zero (`hK4`). The hypotheses `hC0e`, `hC0u` give existence and uniqueness of the normalised level-$n$ structure transported from $P_0$ through a rigidification.
--
--   **The family $\Theta_f$.** For every Noetherian $\pi$-nilpotent $\mathcal{O}$-algebra $B$, $\Theta_f$ sends a triple consisting of an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $\psi : O^{nr} \to B$, a Deligne datum $P$ over $B$ and an element $g \in G$ to a $B$-point of $M$ over $\mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal{O}$. It is natural in $B$ (`hnat`); composing with $\rho(h)$ carries the value at $(x, gh)$ to the value at $(x,g)$ (`hG`); under the dictionary of `hK1`–`hK4` its first component is the fine modular point of the curve with its normalised level structure (`hC2`) and its composite with $p$ is the coarse point of the curve (`hC2c`). A monoid homomorphism $\theta_t : \tilde\Gamma \to G$ is given, and `hC3`, `hC5` (summarised here) state the $\tilde\Gamma$-equivariance of the dictionary together with the effect on normalised level structures and on extra level-$\ell$ subgroups, and conversely that over an algebraically closed field any isomorphism between two curves attached to the same leg comes from some $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$, compatibly with level structures and extra levels.
--
--   **The three appended hypotheses.** `hfib₁` is the one-leg fibre description: for $k$ algebraically closed with $\pi$ nilpotent, a single leg $\psi$, Deligne data $P, P'$ and $g, g' \in G$, one has $\Theta_f((\psi,P),g) = \Theta_f((\psi,P'),g')$ if and only if there is $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ with $g' = \theta_t(\gamma)g$ and $(\psi,P')$ obtained from $(\psi,P)$ by the twisted action of $\iota_0(\gamma)$. `hblind` is leg blindness: for Noetherian $\pi$-nilpotent $C$, triples $x_1, x_2$ and $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$, if the second component of $x_2$ is the pullback of that of $x_1$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$, if the leg of $x_2$ agrees with $\psi_1 \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-v(\iota_0\gamma)}$ on every $y \in O^{nr}$ fixed by $\mathrm{Fr}^{v(\iota_0 z)}$ for all rational scalars $z \in \tilde\Gamma$ with $\theta_t(z) = 1$, and if the $G$-component of $x_2$ is $\theta_t(\gamma)$ times that of $x_1$, then $\Theta_f(x_2) = \Theta_f(x_1)$. `hi'` is twisted invariance: if $x'$ is obtained from $x$ by the twisted action of $\iota_0(\gamma)$ with $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$, then $\Theta_f(x', \theta_t(\gamma)g) = \Theta_f(x,g)$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Under all of the above, for every algebraically closed field $k$ which is an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra with $\pi$ nilpotent in $k$, for all $\mathcal{O}$-algebra maps $\psi, \psi' : O^{nr} \to k$, all Deligne data $P, P'$ over $k$ and all $g, g' \in G$:
--   $$\Theta_f((\psi,P),g) = \Theta_f((\psi',P'),g')$$
--   holds if and only if there exists $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ such that
--
--   (i) $g' = \theta_t(\gamma)\,g$;
--
--   (ii) $P'$ is the pullback of $P$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$, that is, for every full lattice $M$ in $K_0^2$ the line of $P'$ at $M$ is the preimage of the line of $P$ at $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}M$ under the base-changed lattice isomorphism;
--
--   (iii) for every $y \in O^{nr}$ with the property that $\mathrm{Fr}^{v(\iota_0 z)}(y) = y$ for all $z \in \tilde\Gamma$ that are rational scalars and satisfy $\theta_t(z) = 1$, one has $\psi'(y) = \psi\bigl(\mathrm{Fr}^{-v(\iota_0\gamma)}(y)\bigr)$.
--
--   Thus the two legs are allowed to differ, and the condition on them is imposed only on the subring of elements fixed by the Frobenius powers attached to the central, $\theta_t$-trivial elements of $\tilde\Gamma$; on the Deligne data the pullback condition is imposed exactly.
--
--   This is the cross-leg form of the description of the geometric fibres of the fine Čerednik–Drinfeld family $\Theta_f$ on the level-$n$ moduli scheme $M$: it upgrades the one-leg description `hfib₁`, in which both points carry the same unramified leg, to arbitrary pairs of legs, the price being that the legs are compared only on the subring fixed by the Frobenius powers coming from central elements of $\tilde\Gamma$ acting trivially through $\theta_t$. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.fineFamily_eq_iff_exists_mem_levelHom_of_isAlgClosed_heightNormalised_eq_hC5oneLeg`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.fineFamily_eq_iff_exists_mem_levelHom_of_isAlgClosed_heightNormalised_eq_hC5oneLeg), and is the step identifying the geometric points of $M$ over the $\pi$-adic base with the quotient of $\hat\Omega \times G$ by $\tilde\Gamma$ in the $p$-adic uniformisation of the Shimura curve.
